cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset or the measurement protocol in your work, please cite it as below."
title: "Language Advantage Is a Function of the Executor: criteria self-audit and undecidability audit in a seven-language, 81-run study with LLM coders (timetable-ab)"
abstract: >-
  Original proposition: when the executor is an AI large language model, does Lisp
  have a measurable language advantage? Eleven levers were pre-registered and tested
  over seven languages (Common Lisp, JavaScript, Java, Julia, Rust, Python, C++),
  81 runs and 36 invariants, each proven able to go red before it was allowed to pass.
  Results are reported three ways: three claims established, three refuted, and six
  undecidable. The undecidable class is the point of this release: three conditions
  are missing (no weak-executor channel, no exclusive machine or clock alignment, a
  single model family), two further items are incomplete (nine surviving mutants;
  scale limited to 30 ops). See CONDITIONS.md. The dataset does not read "not
  detected" as "not present", does not extrapolate beyond the measured scale, and
  does not delete data.
